6
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,420
 Elevation Control works, okay?

7
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:45,180
 So when a user launches an elevated processor, they try to, ThreatLocker agent will hook that

8
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,140
 into its own elevation flow, so we don't see any UAC prompt or anything.

9
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:53,800
 The ThreatLocker admin will be able to grant that process elevated permissions for a certain

10
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:56,760
 amount of time or whatever, very granular control there.

11
00:00:56,760 --> 00:01:02,580
 And then the ThreatLocker agent on the user's device injects a modified process security token,

12
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:04,580
 which will elevate that process directly.

13
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:09,820
 This is awesome because it avoids things like UAC, which leaves NTLM hashes and stuff like

14
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:13,420
 that in memory, right, in lsas.axe.

15
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:16,700
 It creates a time-bounded elevation, right?

16
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,960
 And it does the elevation to the process rather than to the user.

17
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:22,820
 Really great stuff, but of course, there's always, like, maintenance mode and that sort

18
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:26,740
 of thing if you have to get in there and do a bunch of administrative activities.

19
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:27,940
 Great stuff by ThreatLocker once again.

